# TypeScript Conventions

- `strict` mode is on (`tsconfig.app.json`), including `noUnusedLocals` and `noUnusedParameters` — do not introduce unused variables/params (prefix intentionally-unused destructured values, e.g. `const { [key]: _removed, ...rest } = obj`).
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes/props and `type` for unions, maps, and function signatures, matching existing usage (e.g. `interface User`, `type LogoutOptions`).
- Put shared/domain types (entities returned by the API, DTOs, enums used across features) in `src/types/`. Keep component-local prop types and page-local model types colocated with their file instead.
- Avoid `any`. When the shape of API data is genuinely unknown, type it as `unknown`/`Record<string, unknown>` and narrow explicitly (see `axiosInstance.ts`'s `isPlainObject`), or cast intentionally with a comment explaining why.
- Use `React.FC<Props>` for component typing and explicit event types from React (`React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>`, `React.FormEvent`, MUI's `SelectChangeEvent<T>`) for handlers — do not use untyped `any` event params.
- Wrap `async` calls to context/service functions used as fire-and-forget in `void` (e.g. `void handleUserSelection(id)`) rather than leaving a floating promise or awaiting unnecessarily in non-async callbacks.
- When intentionally suppressing an exhaustive-deps warning, add a one-line comment above the `eslint-disable-next-line` explaining why, as done in `useComparingPageModel.ts`.
